The city's precipitation patterns are dominated by the seasonal monsoon, with the majority of the annual rainfall occurring during the wet season from May through October. During these months, moisture-laden winds from the Shendan Ocean bring frequent afternoon thunderstorms and extended periods of overcast weather, with August typically being the wettest month. The dry season, extending from November through April, is characterized by more stable weather conditions, lower humidity, and significantly less rainfall, though some precipitation occurs in all months due to the city's maritime location.

Minatojima is frequently affected by Eastern Typhoons between July and September, tropical cyclones that develop over the warm waters of the Shendan Ocean and track westward toward the archipelago. These powerful storms bring heavy rainfall, high winds, and dangerous storm surges that have shaped the city's urban planning and infrastructure for centuries. Traditional Sanese architecture in the region incorporated design features to resist typhoon damage, including steeply pitched roofs, heavy timber construction, and buildings oriented to present narrow profiles to the prevailing storm winds. Modern building codes require structures to withstand wind speeds exceeding 200 km/h, and the city maintains an extensive network of storm shelters and evacuation routes.

The typhoon season of 2019 was particularly destructive, even by historical standards. Typhoon Hina, a massive Category 4 storm that struck the region in early September, brought sustained winds of over 200 km/h and dumped more than 400 mm of rain in a 24-hour period. The resulting widespread flooding inundated large portions of the waterfront district, overwhelmed the city's drainage systems, and caused damages exceeding ¥120 billion. The storm prompted a comprehensive review of the city's flood control infrastructure and led to significant investments in improved pumping capacity, elevated building foundations, and expanded green spaces designed to absorb excess rainfall.

== Governance ==
Minatojima operates under a mayor-council system as designated by the Local Autonomy Act of Okaiken, the same governance structure employed by most major cities throughout the nation. The city serves as the capital of [[Seto Prefecture]], hosting the Prefectural Government Building and the Prefectural Assembly, which gives it an administrative importance that extends beyond its municipal boundaries. This dual role as both a local government and a regional administrative center has influenced the city's development, attracting government employees, legal professionals, and businesses that serve the public sector.

The directly elected mayor serves a four-year term and oversees the city's administrative departments, with broad powers to appoint senior officials, propose budgets, and implement policy initiatives. The current mayor, {{nihongo|Hiroshi Koyama|小山博}}, a direct descendant of the historical lords of the Seto Domain, has held office since 2018 and represents a fascinating continuity between the city's feudal past and its democratic present. A member of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken]] who previously served as vice-governor of Seto Prefecture, Koyama has focused his administration on sustainable development, technology industry growth, and the preservation of the city's maritime heritage.

The Minatojima City Council consists of 48 members elected from 12 electoral districts for four-year terms. The council is responsible for enacting local ordinances, approving the city budget, and overseeing mayoral appointments through a system of committee reviews and confirmation votes. Council sessions are held monthly at the Minatojima City Hall, a modernist structure completed in 1988 that has become an architectural landmark in its own right. The council's composition reflects the city's diverse political landscape, with representatives from multiple parties including the Progressive Party, the Liberal Democratic Party, and various smaller parties and independent candidates.

Among the notable former mayors who have shaped the city's development, {{nihongo|Sawada Ichirō|澤田一郎}} (1890–1904) deserves recognition as the first elected mayor who oversaw the initial modernization of urban infrastructure during the tumultuous early years of the Saito era. {{nihongo|Hoshino Kazuo|星野一夫}} (1946–1960) led the difficult post-war reconstruction period, coordinating the massive rebuilding effort that transformed the city from wartime ruins into a modern industrial center. {{nihongo|Takeda Yumi|武田由美}} (1996–2008) broke new ground as the first female mayor of a major Okaiken city and is remembered for promoting the Digital Harbor Initiative that positioned Minatojima for success in the 21st-century economy.

== Demographics ==
As the capital of Seto Prefecture, Minatojima has experienced steady population growth since the 1970s, fueled by its role as a regional administrative and logistics center that attracts migrants from smaller towns and rural areas throughout the prefecture. According to the 2026 census estimate, the city has a population of 1,251,786, making it the largest municipality in the prefecture and the fourth-largest city on Yagikoshu. This population represents a more than sixfold increase since the end of the Great Adonian War, reflecting the city's emergence as a major economic center in the post-war period.

